# documentation_pages/shapes

Measures shapes on a grid, as [area](#area) does.

<a id="area"></a>

## `area`

```knave
(defn area (width height))
(type area (fn (i64 i64) i64))
```

Multiplies two sides, as [perimeter](#perimeter) adds them.

It returns no [option](https://knave-lang.org/docs/prelude/types/#option), and [double](../documentation_pages/lines.md#double) or [square?](#square%3F) help. An [if](https://knave-lang.org/docs/prelude/#if) decides
nothing here, and [missing] names nothing. `[perimeter]` is code,
[a website](https://example.com) is a link, and [two words], [a][b] and [open
stay.

```knave
(area 2 3)
=> 6
```

```knave
(area 1 1)
```

<a id="perimeter"></a>

## `perimeter`

```knave
(defn perimeter (width height))
```

No documentation.

<a id="square?"></a>

## `square?`

```knave
(defn square? (width height))
```

Tells whether both sides match, as the private [helper] does not.

```rust
width == height
```

````markdown
```
(square? 1 1)
```
````

```knave
```sh
```

```knave
(square? 2 2)
```

## Re-exported names

- [`double`](../documentation_pages/lines.md#double)

# documentation_pages/lines

<a id="double"></a>

## `double`

```knave
(defn double (x))
```

Doubles [x], which [area] cannot see.

